{
  "summary": "The TUI file (pipeline/tui.py) has grown to 4,082 lines with at least 5 distinct concerns tangled together: (1) 10+ modal dialog classes (RejectModal, NewBoardModal, AnswerQuestionsModal, NewFeatureModal, EditDoneScriptModal, MoveFeatureModal, RunScriptModal, ConfirmModal, UnifiedEditModal — ~600 lines), (2) sidebar panes like SettingsPane, ScriptsPane, AgentStatusWidget (~225 lines), (3) standalone helper functions for formatting/markdown/CLI (~225 lines), (4) lock management and entry-point boilerplate (~30 lines), and (5) the 2,300+ line PipelineApp god-class that mixes board rendering, feature data loading, pipeline orchestration (plan/implement/debate workers), server-push communication, keybinding dispatch, and the _check_for_changes polling loop that stats every feature file each cycle. A natural decomposition would extract modals into tui_modals.py, panes into tui_panes.py, and helpers into tui_helpers.py as a first pass — these are purely presentational and have minimal coupling to PipelineApp state. The harder but higher-value refactor is splitting PipelineApp itself: extracting a BoardDataStore that owns feature loading, caching, and change-detection (replacing the per-file stat loop with filesystem watches or a hash-based dirty flag), and a PipelineOrchestrator that owns the async workers for plan/implement/debate/scripts so PipelineApp becomes a thin shell wiring UI events to these services. This layered approach lets each piece be tested independently and makes _check_for_changes' scalability problem fixable in isolation."
}
